 ## Private Cache
 
-Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. Before admitting a completed sheet, evict enough existing sheets to remain within that bound. Temporary render output is kept separately and removed on completion or failure. Eviction never deletes source footage or changes job-ticket semantics.
+Cached JPEG data may occupy at most 12 GiB. The cache uses a 2Q replacement policy with two queues:
 
-The eviction policy is still open. Candidates are LRU, frequency-based eviction with aging, and 2Q. LRU has little bookkeeping and follows recent work quickly but is vulnerable to sequential scans. Aged frequency counts protect repeatedly popular clips but add counter maintenance and can lag a project switch. 2Q separates first-use entries from reused entries, resisting scans at the cost of extra queues and a queue-size parameter. None changes the external interface, queue concurrency, or disk budget.
+**Admission queue (A1):** Newly admitted sheets enter A1 at the tail. A1 is evicted FIFO — the oldest-admitted entry leaves first. A1 has a soft target of 25% of the cache limit (3 GiB). A1 absorbs sequential-scan sheets; any sheet accessed only once stays in A1 until evicted.
+
+**Main queue (Am):** A sheet promoted from A1 on a second access enters Am at the tail. Am is evicted LRU — the least recently accessed entry leaves first. Am has a soft target of 75% of the cache limit (9 GiB). Am holds the reused comparison sheets.
+
+**Size targets are soft:** If Am holds fewer than its 9 GiB target, the slack is available to A1, and vice versa. Total cached data must not exceed 12 GiB at any time.
+
+**Admission:** When a completed sheet is ready for admission and the total cache is at capacity, evict from the A1 head first. If A1 is empty, evict from the Am head. Then admit the new sheet to A1. No sheet is admitted directly to Am.
+
+**Promotion:** When a cache hit occurs for a sheet in A1, move it immediately to the Am tail. The sheet's byte count leaves A1's total and enters Am's. Promotion never triggers additional eviction — the sheet was already counted against the total.
+
+**Eviction never removes source footage, changes job-ticket state, or affects any external interface.**
+
+Temporary render output is kept separately from the two queues and removed on completion or failure.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Eviction policy | 2Q (A1 admission queue + Am main queue) | LRU; frequency-based eviction with aging | See [decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md](decisions/cache-eviction-policy.md). LRU fails scan resistance structurally; frequency-based aging ties scan resistance to a decay rate, coupling it to adaptation speed. 2Q provides unconditional structural scan resistance without per-entry counters. |
+| A1 / Am size split | 25% A1 / 75% Am, soft targets summing to 12 GiB | 50/50 split; fully dynamic rebalancing | 25/75 is the conventional starting point from the 2Q literature. At 2 MiB average sheet size it gives Am capacity for roughly 4,500 reused sheets. The split is configurable at startup for tuning; a fully dynamic split adds rebalancing logic without a workload signal to drive it. |
+
